bin/
# The furrow the build fetches, and the copy it stages for go:embed. Both are
# six megabytes of somebody else's release; internal/furrowbin/pin.json is the
# committed, auditable half and these are rebuilt from it by `make furrow`.
third_party/
internal/furrowbin/cache/furrow-*.gz
.obs/
*.log
# macOS Finder leaves this litter behind, and one copy was committed once.
.DS_Store
.claude/
!internal/skills/testdata/**/.claude/
/codeaf
bench-results/
.codeaf/
!internal/skills/testdata/**/.codeaf/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.aux
harnesses/
bench/swarm/results/
# The timing report is diagnostic evidence for one run. Several sessions share
# this checkout, and an untracked file at its root is what another lane's
# `git add` sweeps up.
/test-report.json
# The prompt-diet bench writes its evidence and its builds OUTSIDE any checkout
# (~/bench-diet-out and ~/bench-diet-build), because a cell's workspace nested
# inside this repository hands the model the harness instead of its fixture.
# This line is the backstop for an --out somebody points back in here anyway.
bench/prompt-diet/out/
# The manuals are generated for packed release builds. Their Markdown folders
# are the tracked source; committing these shared binaries causes every branch
# that documents a feature to conflict with every other one.
internal/manual/chat.pack.gz
internal/manual/pages.pack.gz
# The engine writes the ids of the workspace it opens into .furrow/ — family-id
# and workspace-id, which name this machine and not this repository. Every
# checkout a run has lived in therefore carries one, and an untracked directory
# at the root is what the next lane's `git add -A` sweeps up: two of these were
# committed that way on #1173 and had to be taken back out.
.furrow/
bench/idle-surface/runs/
work/
